  "slug": "editing-preserves-7479",
  "summary": "Recent preprints extend AAV-based gene replacement and editing to cardiac and mitochondrial disease models with functional readouts in vivo.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is in vivo genetic correction in mouse disease models using AAV-based or compact editing delivery. Two preprints apply AAV gene replacement to mitochondrial disease: LRPPRC replacement in heart and muscle knockout mice restored cardiac expression, preserved OXPHOS, prevented cardiomyopathy and extended survival, while neonatal Polg replacement preserved respiratory chain activity, delayed motor deficits and extended lifespan. A single-AAV adenine base editor with a compact cardiomyocyte-specific promoter corrects an MYBPC3 mutation and reverses hypercontractility and hypertrophy. An engineered compact PAMless ParTasR variant with TREX2 co-expression improves mammalian editing efficiency and corrects a tyrosinemia mouse model. The RepoV enhancer-reporter study is a distinct methodological contribution in human iPSC-derived neural organoids. What is new is the pairing of compact delivery architectures with functional rescue readouts across cardiac, muscle and metabolic disease models.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-20"
}
